messages:
sophia: Heads up — a customer on Node 18 is seeing their requests hang when we retry around 10s. They say aborting the original request still leaves retries running and they get duplicate downstream calls. Repro attached.

jamal: Can you paste the repro and the SDK version?

sophia: SDK @ 0.14.2, node 18.17.0. Minimal repro:
```js
// simplified repro
const controller = new AbortController();
fetch('https://api.redwood.ai/v1/generate', {signal: controller.signal, method: 'POST', body: '{}' })
  .catch(err => console.log('first fetch err', err.message));

setTimeout(() => {
  controller.abort();
}, 1200);
// SDK's internal retry kicks in and issues a new fetch with the SAME signal object
// due to reuse in our retry wrapper -> abort affects both.
```

kevin: Oof. That sounds like the classic signal reuse bug — if the client reuses the same AbortSignal for subsequent attempts, aborting the controller cancels the whole retry chain or causes a race where the old socket is left. Did they see ERR_SOCKET_CLOSED or just duplicate downstream requests?

sophia: They saw duplicate POSTs to their webhook (two payloads ~1.2s apart), then an eventual 502 from our edge. No ERR_SOCKET_CLOSED in their app logs.

jamal: Interesting. If our retry code constructs a new Request but copies the signal reference, and if undici/Node treats reuse differently between node versions, we can get timeouts but also a disconnected write that still reaches the backend. We should isolate whether the SDK is cloning the signal or accidentally passing the same controller.

maria: I pushed a quick patch in a local branch that ensures each retry gets a fresh AbortController derived from a parent timeout, i.e. create child controllers per attempt and transfer signal only for the per-attempt lifetime. That avoids reuse. Can you try the patched bundle with the repro?

sophia: Trying now against their repro environment...

support-bot: Incident created: INC-2026-45 — "ts-sdk-retry-signal-interop". Assigning to @jamal and @maria. :white_check_mark:

sophia: With maria's branch the duplicates stopped in the repro. Single POST and then a clean aborted attempt that doesn't hit their webhook.

jamal: Great. Two followups: 1) We need a unit test that asserts we create distinct signals per attempt, 2) check undici/node interactions across Node 16/18/20 because behavior differs (fetch polyfills and undici handle AbortSignal semantics slightly differently).

maria: Agreed. I will add a test that mocks fetch and asserts the callback gets different signal.identity per attempt, plus an integration run on Node 16/18/20. Also logging in SDK to include attempt id and whether signal was shared for easier customer debugging.

kevin: For support: please tell the customer the root cause is our retry wrapper reusing an AbortSignal object between attempts on some Node/undici combos. We're shipping a patch to 0.14.x and will include a patch release. Offer to validate their workload with the patched bundle.

sophia: Noted. Customer is ok trying the patched build. I'll follow up with a temporary build and escalate a patch release.

jamal: One more note — also audit any code paths that pass a user-supplied signal into the SDK. If we attach user signals to every retry, we need to doc the semantics: user-supplied controller will cancel entire operation (expected), but SDK-managed per-attempt signals must be unique.

maria: I'll also add docs and a changelog entry clarifying that passing a signal cancels the whole operation and we won't reuse it across internal retries.

kevin: Perfect. Mark this resolved when release candidate is published. :thumbsup:
